1
00:00:12,827 --> 00:00:32,581
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of the attitude that one should have when beginning to look into the suttas, to start the path, to try to understand what the Buddha is saying, is that one must understand that you don't know.

2
00:00:35,083 --> 00:00:36,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that is pretty much prerequisite.

3
00:00:37,513 --> 00:00:39,514
[SPEAKER_00]: But that seems to be overlooked, pretty much.

4
00:00:40,115 --> 00:00:43,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Because most people start off with, I know quite a bit already.

5
00:00:43,917 --> 00:00:44,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

6
00:00:44,278 --> 00:00:45,759
[SPEAKER_01]: They start with, I don't know.

7
00:00:46,519 --> 00:00:51,402
[SPEAKER_01]: But as soon as they get to know something, that becomes, oh, now I know.

8
00:00:53,524 --> 00:00:54,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody starts, I don't know.

9
00:00:55,265 --> 00:01:01,929
[SPEAKER_01]: But the first port of call I become familiar with, oh, I read this sutta, or I heard from that teacher.

10
00:01:02,310 --> 00:01:02,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

11
00:01:02,950 --> 00:01:05,312
[SPEAKER_01]: And now that already gave me a basis, which is inevitable.

12
00:01:05,332 --> 00:01:06,032
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to happen.

13
00:01:06,232 --> 00:01:07,233
[SPEAKER_01]: You become more familiar.

14
00:01:08,786 --> 00:01:15,192
[SPEAKER_01]: But the slightest sort of familiarity then gets conceived as knowledge, misconceived as knowledge.

15
00:01:16,033 --> 00:01:22,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So now I'm familiar with it, and you immediately start assuming that that's now you know what dhamma is.

16
00:01:23,620 --> 00:01:34,329
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, again, to give an example, say you don't know anything, you heard about Buddhism, seems interesting, you want to learn to meditate, want to free yourself from suffering, maybe in whatever vague terms,

17
00:01:35,361 --> 00:01:48,044
[SPEAKER_01]: You relate to that thought, and then you go, I don't know, and listen to a talk from some Theravāda monk, and then you start reading the suttas, and now you know more, you're more familiar, you've got more information.

18
00:01:48,484 --> 00:01:50,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Information is not the knowledge.

19
00:01:50,765 --> 00:01:53,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, have you understood it?

20
00:01:53,065 --> 00:01:59,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, well, yes, I understood it to some degree, because I make sense out of it, I can explain to somebody else.

21
00:01:59,787 --> 00:02:01,407
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine, but that is mundane.

22
00:02:02,407 --> 00:02:03,468
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the understanding.

23
00:02:03,508 --> 00:02:05,188
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not even the beginning of understanding.

24
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:13,646
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't forsake the mundane idea of understanding, you will not then develop the supramundane, which is the understanding.

25
00:02:14,547 --> 00:02:16,609
[SPEAKER_01]: The understanding that frees you from suffering.

26
00:02:17,109 --> 00:02:18,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that is the beginning of understanding.

27
00:02:19,411 --> 00:02:23,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing the Four Noble Truths, as those very same suttas describe.

28
00:02:23,895 --> 00:02:30,100
[SPEAKER_01]: So, here you are, thinking you're understanding, because you're familiar with it, you read it so many times, you have all the information, all the etymology.

29
00:02:31,133 --> 00:02:34,635
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're overlooking the fact that, yeah, but do I know the way out of suffering?

30
00:02:35,336 --> 00:02:42,900
[SPEAKER_01]: All of this is great, but have... if I were to understand one sutta, let alone many, I would be at least a sotāpanna.

31
00:02:44,201 --> 00:02:46,242
[SPEAKER_01]: If I were to understand in the way that matters.

32
00:02:46,342 --> 00:02:47,583
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the understanding.

33
00:02:48,463 --> 00:02:53,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you use the same word for understanding, as in if you... well, that's understanding, but also my...

34
00:02:54,980 --> 00:02:57,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Familiarity with of information is also understanding.

35
00:02:58,541 --> 00:03:02,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then you will not push yourself into that understanding that will free you from suffering.

36
00:03:02,863 --> 00:03:11,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you somehow believe that understanding of information pertains to understanding from a way out of suffering.

37
00:03:12,566 --> 00:03:13,146
[SPEAKER_01]: And it doesn't.

38
00:03:14,607 --> 00:03:19,929
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no linear, uh, guaranteed, sort of, step by step.

39
00:03:19,989 --> 00:03:23,710
[SPEAKER_01]: You read this sutta, then read this sutta, then read that sutta, and then you sort of...

40
00:03:24,562 --> 00:03:26,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, how did you become a Sotāpanna?

41
00:03:26,143 --> 00:03:27,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by understanding what Sotāpanna is.

42
00:03:28,764 --> 00:03:34,846
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you need some information to start, as in to know where to think, in what direction to go.

43
00:03:35,906 --> 00:03:50,032
[SPEAKER_01]: But, unless you make that effort, regardless of the information, and certainly not impeded by thinking understanding of information is the understanding of the freedom from suffering, then you will make that effort.

44
00:03:53,618 --> 00:03:54,600
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's all you need to do.

45
00:03:54,680 --> 00:03:58,385
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about not reading the suttas because I end up thinking I understood.

46
00:03:58,645 --> 00:04:00,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's another problem now.

47
00:04:01,449 --> 00:04:06,697
[SPEAKER_01]: So how do you know what to understand if you're not reading the instruction from the Buddha?

48
00:04:06,737 --> 00:04:07,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

49
00:04:07,937 --> 00:04:20,124
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you want to do is keep reading the suttas, keep listening to the talks, but keep reflecting upon it to the point of, if you were to understand it, you will be free from suffering.

50
00:04:20,144 --> 00:04:27,288
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not being free from suffering, as in free from liability to suffer, not just, oh yes, it helped me deal with something.

51
00:04:27,308 --> 00:04:27,689
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

52
00:04:28,329 --> 00:04:34,633
[SPEAKER_01]: It helped me not experience suffering amidst the same circumstances that used to cause me to suffer.

53
00:04:35,376 --> 00:04:36,397
[SPEAKER_01]: That should be the norm.

54
00:04:36,457 --> 00:04:37,977
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the bare minimum, by the way.

55
00:04:39,538 --> 00:04:43,580
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only reason people don't seek that as a bare minimum is because they deny that that's a bare minimum.

56
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:52,524
[SPEAKER_01]: They lower the bar even more and say, no, familiarity of understanding, familiarity of information, and I get my head around it, that's already the minimum.

57
00:04:52,644 --> 00:04:53,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's not.

58
00:04:53,465 --> 00:04:54,906
[SPEAKER_01]: The minimum is being free from suffering.

59
00:04:54,926 --> 00:05:01,649
[SPEAKER_01]: The minimum is not being still subjected to possibility of, you know, peril, if you are reborn in a lower realm.

60
00:05:02,149 --> 00:05:03,750
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the minimum is the right view.

61
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:09,771
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, one who has the right view, is the one who sees the dhamma.

62
00:05:10,331 --> 00:05:12,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, without the right view, you are not seeing the dhamma.

63
00:05:12,331 --> 00:05:18,653
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you be saying your familiarity of information that talks about dhamma, is understanding of the dhamma to some extent?

64
00:05:18,693 --> 00:05:18,793
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

65
00:05:18,873 --> 00:05:20,233
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be.

66
00:05:20,253 --> 00:05:21,593
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know what is right and wrong.

67
00:05:21,613 --> 00:05:22,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

68
00:05:22,134 --> 00:05:22,634
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know.

69
00:05:22,654 --> 00:05:24,354
[SPEAKER_00]: What teaching is right, what is wrong.

70
00:05:24,494 --> 00:05:24,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

71
00:05:25,114 --> 00:05:26,014
[SPEAKER_01]: I've got no idea.

72
00:05:26,815 --> 00:05:27,975
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the problem.

73
00:05:30,152 --> 00:05:34,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to keep reading the suttas, you want to keep listening to the talks, but you also want to be reflecting.

74
00:05:35,075 --> 00:05:41,318
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're not being free from suffering, it's due to... it's only possible because of the two... there can only be two reasons for it.

75
00:05:42,759 --> 00:05:50,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Either the instruction that you're listening is wrong, or you're not properly attending to it.

76
00:05:50,963 --> 00:05:51,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Or both.

77
00:05:52,764 --> 00:05:53,585
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess there is a third.

78
00:05:54,513 --> 00:05:55,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

79
00:05:55,894 --> 00:05:56,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Or both.

80
00:05:58,355 --> 00:05:59,236
[SPEAKER_01]: But fundamentally...

81
00:05:59,276 --> 00:06:01,717
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you kind of question.

82
00:06:02,678 --> 00:06:04,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so is this instruction correct?

83
00:06:04,459 --> 00:06:11,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I can't quite tell, but I can use my intelligence to narrow it down, which is least likely to be wrong, the instruction.

84
00:06:12,703 --> 00:06:20,768
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's always like lean towards the suttas, talks either about the suttas, they will push you into reading suttas, because that is, again, you don't know for sure the suttas are right either.

85
00:06:21,510 --> 00:06:24,595
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to start somewhere, and that is least likely to be wrong.

86
00:06:25,316 --> 00:06:26,377
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the best you can do.

87
00:06:27,619 --> 00:06:28,360
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, not...

88
00:06:28,420 --> 00:06:31,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so now, the suttas are least likely to be wrong.

89
00:06:31,525 --> 00:06:32,526
[SPEAKER_01]: I believe in the suttas.

90
00:06:32,867 --> 00:06:33,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Done deal.

91
00:06:33,728 --> 00:06:35,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, but now you have to start working.

92
00:06:37,275 --> 00:06:38,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, you're very familiar.

93
00:06:38,515 --> 00:06:40,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, you're making lots of sense with them.

94
00:06:40,876 --> 00:06:46,337
[SPEAKER_01]: But are you being freed from liability to suffer, even before the suffering?

95
00:06:46,617 --> 00:06:51,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that understanding resulting in you being unable to experience dukkha?

96
00:06:52,619 --> 00:06:55,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like hiding from the dukkha or avoiding things.

97
00:06:55,619 --> 00:06:55,759
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

98
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,400
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm simply unable, even amidst things that used to cause me to suffer.

99
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:01,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind cannot experience it.

100
00:07:02,181 --> 00:07:05,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the water just falls off those leaves.

101
00:07:07,225 --> 00:07:11,449
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in this sutta, Majjhima Nikaya 70, Buddha says, I do not say that final knowledge, actual knowledge, is achieved all at once.

102
00:07:30,552 --> 00:07:36,694
[SPEAKER_00]: On the contrary, final knowledge is achieved by gradual training, by gradual practice, by gradual progress.

103
00:07:37,815 --> 00:07:39,035
[SPEAKER_00]: And how is this done?

104
00:07:40,036 --> 00:07:41,776
[SPEAKER_00]: One who has faith in a teacher...

105
00:07:42,376 --> 00:07:43,817
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the gradual training?

106
00:07:43,857 --> 00:07:44,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

107
00:07:45,337 --> 00:07:47,278
[SPEAKER_00]: One who has faith in a teacher visits him.

108
00:07:47,558 --> 00:07:47,658
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

109
00:07:47,998 --> 00:07:49,039
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already what I said.

110
00:07:49,299 --> 00:07:56,702
[SPEAKER_01]: So, faith in the teacher corresponds to, okay, who's least likely to be wrong based on my discerning capabilities.

111
00:07:57,742 --> 00:07:59,603
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I don't know where out of suffering, but

112
00:08:00,596 --> 00:08:02,977
[SPEAKER_01]: I have my intelligence intact, I can think for myself.

113
00:08:03,717 --> 00:08:05,437
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which is least likely to be wrong?

114
00:08:06,757 --> 00:08:07,818
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you seek out.

115
00:08:09,118 --> 00:08:18,300
[SPEAKER_01]: When you find out what is least likely to be wrong, what seems to be pertaining to that which might free you from suffering, you put faith in it.

116
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:26,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Not for its own sake, not as its own goal, but just faith in terms of, okay, I'll apply this.

117
00:08:26,022 --> 00:08:27,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, like choosing a medicine.

118
00:08:29,592 --> 00:08:36,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can... oh, this... this is least... this is most promising medicine, but you will never know, unless you start applying it and following the instructions.

119
00:08:37,375 --> 00:08:42,537
[SPEAKER_01]: A particular diet, a particular lifestyle, avoiding certain things, so the medicine can start to work.

120
00:08:43,397 --> 00:08:45,018
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then, you need to wait.

121
00:08:45,478 --> 00:08:47,819
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to keep doing it, gradually, until then...

122
00:08:47,859 --> 00:08:48,379
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

123
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:54,782
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like... the measure of medicine working is then the measure of, ah, I'm healing from suffering.

124
00:08:55,162 --> 00:08:57,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Healing from the possibility to suffer.

125
00:08:59,342 --> 00:09:00,483
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's the true freedom.

126
00:09:01,223 --> 00:09:06,808
[SPEAKER_00]: When you know that you can't suffer, even amidst things that would make everybody else suffer.

127
00:09:08,387 --> 00:09:12,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you learn to focus on your nostrils, or you learn to do this, the quick to respond.

128
00:09:13,111 --> 00:09:13,231
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

129
00:09:13,791 --> 00:09:16,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Simply, you're abandoned craving through understanding.

130
00:09:16,934 --> 00:09:19,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So you're unable to suffer, regardless of the circumstances.

131
00:09:20,737 --> 00:09:31,066
[SPEAKER_01]: To the point that the Buddha said, if you're an arahant, you would have, you could have robbers chopping up your limbs, one by one, like utter savages, and you will not experience dukkha on that account.

132
00:09:32,527 --> 00:09:36,290
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not hate them, you will not have any aversion in your mind towards them.

133
00:09:37,756 --> 00:09:44,959
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the gradual teaching begins with that faith that is a prerequisite for the application of effort.

134
00:09:45,619 --> 00:09:49,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Not faith that's a prerequisite for this superstitious belief.

135
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:58,223
[SPEAKER_01]: That now I chant the suttas, and I read the suttas, and I bow to the suttas, and I have the unwavering faith that the Buddha was right, although I don't know for myself.

136
00:09:58,804 --> 00:09:58,944
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

137
00:09:59,164 --> 00:10:00,424
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the faith we're talking about.

138
00:10:00,644 --> 00:10:02,525
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the faith you have in a doctor.

139
00:10:03,636 --> 00:10:10,720
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, there's some proper quacks, then there's some somebody who seems more knowledgeable, then somebody who seems more knowledgeable, and wise as well.

140
00:10:12,220 --> 00:10:13,841
[SPEAKER_01]: So you choose wisely yourself.

141
00:10:14,582 --> 00:10:15,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one?

142
00:10:15,462 --> 00:10:17,943
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to apply some medicine.

143
00:10:17,963 --> 00:10:19,024
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to start somewhere.

144
00:10:19,704 --> 00:10:26,768
[SPEAKER_01]: And then maybe say, okay, this doctor helped me to some extent, but have I applied the medication correctly?

145
00:10:27,488 --> 00:10:28,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Have I really understood

146
00:10:30,849 --> 00:10:39,597
[SPEAKER_01]: So, always twofold.

147
00:10:39,857 --> 00:10:46,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, the instruction, but not just, oh, he might be saying wrong things, but have I actually really understood this to full extent?

148
00:10:48,744 --> 00:10:51,467
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's even what the Buddha himself did before he became the Buddha.

149
00:10:52,548 --> 00:10:54,930
[SPEAKER_01]: When he was with different teachers, he would then go and

150
00:10:56,182 --> 00:10:58,983
[SPEAKER_01]: You present back to them what he understood, and there we go.

151
00:10:59,003 --> 00:11:00,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, that's exactly what I'm teaching.

152
00:11:01,163 --> 00:11:02,024
[SPEAKER_01]: You are equal to me.

153
00:11:02,064 --> 00:11:02,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Come, sit.

154
00:11:02,804 --> 00:11:04,404
[SPEAKER_01]: We lead this community together.

155
00:11:04,745 --> 00:11:10,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Your understanding is exactly where I'm... And the Buddha says, OK, thank you, but that's not enough for me.

156
00:11:11,286 --> 00:11:12,867
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, have you understood that?

157
00:11:13,147 --> 00:11:13,667
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

158
00:11:13,707 --> 00:11:19,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you choose, oh, this is wrong, you want to know why it's wrong.

159
00:11:19,049 --> 00:11:23,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, say you start following some doctor, some dhammatalk, whatever,

160
00:11:25,179 --> 00:11:28,940
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you're not being free from suffering, and then you decide it's wrong, because now you find something better.

161
00:11:29,960 --> 00:11:36,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Be that as it may, do you know how, why, to what extent was this wrong?

162
00:11:37,381 --> 00:11:40,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you understood it, you would know that it's wrong.

163
00:11:41,442 --> 00:11:46,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are you just dismissing it as wrong, because it stopped inspiring you, because you got lazy, or whatever.

164
00:11:47,383 --> 00:11:47,983
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what I mean.

165
00:11:48,003 --> 00:11:51,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's right or wrong, needs to be understood as right or as wrong.

166
00:11:52,449 --> 00:11:55,810
[SPEAKER_01]: And it can be understood if you keep applying that effort to understand the point.

167
00:11:57,951 --> 00:11:59,112
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the instruction here?

168
00:12:00,132 --> 00:12:01,052
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the goal here?

169
00:12:01,232 --> 00:12:02,213
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the teaching here?

170
00:12:02,293 --> 00:12:03,493
[SPEAKER_00]: What are the implications here?

171
00:12:04,554 --> 00:12:09,375
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of that you can develop or even invest effort if you haven't had faith to begin with.

172
00:12:09,395 --> 00:12:14,997
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're just looking for faults in all the doctors, yeah, that seems pretty good, but meh...

173
00:12:15,698 --> 00:12:16,338
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not sure.

174
00:12:16,798 --> 00:12:17,298
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not sure.

175
00:12:17,378 --> 00:12:17,899
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not sure.

176
00:12:18,059 --> 00:12:18,959
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not applying anything.

177
00:12:20,728 --> 00:12:25,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're truly in a position to choose right and wrong, you wouldn't need doctors to be helping you.

178
00:12:26,432 --> 00:12:30,274
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why first and foremost is, wait, I need help.

179
00:12:31,415 --> 00:12:32,836
[SPEAKER_01]: I am not free from suffering.

180
00:12:34,497 --> 00:12:41,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me not think that I know, when I don't, because if I do know, I would be free from suffering and I wouldn't need any instruction.

181
00:12:43,423 --> 00:12:49,487
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, time might be short, so don't spend too much time picking and choosing, but instead discern

182
00:12:50,708 --> 00:12:51,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Apply.

183
00:12:52,090 --> 00:12:52,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Reflect.

184
00:12:53,352 --> 00:12:53,732
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah.

185
00:12:53,752 --> 00:12:58,698
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a person could go, you know, pretty much anywhere, really, you know, some monastery.

186
00:12:59,800 --> 00:13:01,221
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, yes.

187
00:13:01,462 --> 00:13:04,064
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have to figure out, is it right or is it wrong?

188
00:13:04,104 --> 00:13:08,688
[SPEAKER_00]: But even if you understand that it's wrong, that would be to your benefit.

189
00:13:09,028 --> 00:13:11,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that would be your rightness.

190
00:13:11,451 --> 00:13:12,311
[SPEAKER_00]: This is why it's wrong.

191
00:13:12,672 --> 00:13:12,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

192
00:13:13,332 --> 00:13:14,573
[SPEAKER_00]: It would benefit you, actually.

193
00:13:14,753 --> 00:13:15,374
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's what I mean.

194
00:13:15,394 --> 00:13:18,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, even when I say to people, sometimes they ask for a monastery to go.

195
00:13:18,617 --> 00:13:26,044
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, really, all you need is a monastery where, like, say, they will not be forcing views and books on you.

196
00:13:27,265 --> 00:13:33,755
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, if you read anything that deviates from this, you'll be expelled, because there are places like that.

197
00:13:34,216 --> 00:13:37,762
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, if they're all... he's into suttas, or he's into whatever...

198
00:13:38,883 --> 00:13:44,647
[SPEAKER_01]: This teacher or that teacher, as long as, I suppose, Buddhist, that's fine.

199
00:13:44,707 --> 00:13:48,469
[SPEAKER_01]: If they let you study on your own, do your own study, that's fine.

200
00:13:48,870 --> 00:13:50,591
[SPEAKER_01]: And if the place keeps the vinaya.

201
00:13:50,731 --> 00:13:51,511
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need more.

202
00:13:52,092 --> 00:13:58,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if everything else is, oh yeah, they have these views, and senior monks have such views, and wrong views, none of that is your problem.

203
00:13:59,203 --> 00:14:04,127
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, now you're worrying about the state of the medical profession in general.

204
00:14:04,507 --> 00:14:04,647
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

205
00:14:04,927 --> 00:14:07,629
[SPEAKER_01]: You only need a doctor that knows that you can apply.

206
00:14:08,050 --> 00:14:11,993
[SPEAKER_01]: If you can't find the one that knows directly, well, find the instruction from those who knew.

207
00:14:13,834 --> 00:14:16,116
[SPEAKER_01]: And keep... need environment to keep applying it.

208
00:14:16,156 --> 00:14:22,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Environment where you can keep the vinaya, have enough solitude, and keep studying to the point of developing understanding.

209
00:14:22,941 --> 00:14:23,621
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what means study.

210
00:14:23,641 --> 00:14:24,482
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't mean just study.

211
00:14:25,122 --> 00:14:25,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

212
00:14:25,803 --> 00:14:26,744
[SPEAKER_01]: And collect information.

213
00:14:27,124 --> 00:14:28,545
[SPEAKER_01]: But make effort to reflect.

214
00:14:29,322 --> 00:14:33,426
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that point of view, many of the monasteries would meet the criteria.

215
00:14:33,526 --> 00:14:42,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you will have pointless tasks and duties, but none of that is really a fundamental issue.

216
00:14:43,154 --> 00:14:48,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless, of course, you spend 20 hours of your day doing meaningless things.

217
00:14:48,099 --> 00:14:48,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

218
00:14:50,101 --> 00:14:53,544
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just continue with that gradual... Yeah, so the faith...

219
00:14:53,564 --> 00:14:55,966
[SPEAKER_00]: He visits, visits that person that he has faith in.

220
00:14:56,511 --> 00:14:56,971
[SPEAKER_01]: If he can.

221
00:14:57,291 --> 00:14:58,472
[SPEAKER_01]: If not, he googles it.

222
00:14:58,892 --> 00:15:00,012
[SPEAKER_00]: He pays respects to him.

223
00:15:01,453 --> 00:15:03,874
[SPEAKER_00]: He gives ear.

224
00:15:04,094 --> 00:15:05,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Gives ear means what I said.

225
00:15:05,615 --> 00:15:06,655
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't look for faults.

226
00:15:06,675 --> 00:15:07,295
[SPEAKER_01]: It's full stop.

227
00:15:09,276 --> 00:15:13,698
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to learn how not to look, how not to confuse.

228
00:15:15,238 --> 00:15:21,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, looking for faults tends to be confused for discriminating.

229
00:15:23,265 --> 00:15:26,447
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I tell you don't look for faults, okay, so I must stop discriminating.

230
00:15:26,527 --> 00:15:26,647
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

231
00:15:27,508 --> 00:15:29,710
[SPEAKER_01]: You still look right is right, wrong is wrong.

232
00:15:29,750 --> 00:15:31,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to discern, see for yourself.

233
00:15:31,551 --> 00:15:32,632
[SPEAKER_01]: You just don't look for faults.

234
00:15:33,192 --> 00:15:35,934
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't make your discrimination personal.

235
00:15:37,015 --> 00:15:38,296
[SPEAKER_01]: And you won't be looking for faults.

236
00:15:38,456 --> 00:15:40,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's really what fault finding is.

237
00:15:40,857 --> 00:15:43,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Your sole problem is to understand what's being said.

238
00:15:44,640 --> 00:15:46,441
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it's wrong, to understand why it's wrong.

239
00:15:46,802 --> 00:15:48,563
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it feels right, to understand why it's right.

240
00:15:50,910 --> 00:15:53,551
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't make it personal, you will not look for faults.

241
00:15:54,171 --> 00:15:59,132
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you're saying something wrong, I will not be, ah, he's wrong, he's wrong.

242
00:15:59,813 --> 00:16:02,193
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's just, what's being said doesn't fit.

243
00:16:03,674 --> 00:16:06,214
[SPEAKER_01]: And I understand to this extent that it doesn't fit.

244
00:16:07,615 --> 00:16:14,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you might tell me back that I misunderstood you, and then you'd explain, in and out, but maybe you will then learn something.

245
00:16:15,377 --> 00:16:18,178
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I just look for faults, means I haven't understood.

246
00:16:19,010 --> 00:16:19,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Why you are wrong?

247
00:16:20,471 --> 00:16:21,471
[SPEAKER_01]: The wrongness of that.

248
00:16:23,452 --> 00:16:25,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I put myself first.

249
00:16:26,814 --> 00:16:29,135
[SPEAKER_01]: See, by looking for faults in you, I imply I'm better.

250
00:16:30,275 --> 00:16:30,876
[SPEAKER_01]: It's implicit.

251
00:16:31,056 --> 00:16:33,157
[SPEAKER_01]: It's automatic.

252
00:16:33,197 --> 00:16:37,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Even what you're expressing, if it's wrong, it's not a personal thing for you either.

253
00:16:38,139 --> 00:16:40,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Or if it is for you, it doesn't need to be my personal thing.

254
00:16:41,321 --> 00:16:43,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Like you might be taking personally, but it's not my problem.

255
00:16:45,213 --> 00:16:55,659
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't look for faults means be open to hear what's being said without the kind of, sort of, a bit of a reserve, like, hmm... You have no criteria what fits and what's truth.

256
00:16:55,719 --> 00:17:02,542
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to really commit to the instruction and understand it fully, through and through.

257
00:17:03,083 --> 00:17:07,705
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if it were wrong, it would become evident without needing to make it personal.

258
00:17:08,626 --> 00:17:11,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, oh, the instruction was wrong because there is such and such discrepancy.

259
00:17:12,408 --> 00:17:13,088
[SPEAKER_01]: And I understood it.

260
00:17:13,628 --> 00:17:14,709
[SPEAKER_01]: And I understood the extent of it.

261
00:17:16,132 --> 00:17:18,173
[SPEAKER_01]: And I can explain it, because I understood it.

262
00:17:18,754 --> 00:17:19,474
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I used to do.

263
00:17:19,855 --> 00:17:24,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, go to the library in the monastery, like, books of any kind.

264
00:17:25,238 --> 00:17:33,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Not any kind, but, I mean, like, Buddhism, but from all sorts of the very broad spectrum of teachers and traditions.

265
00:17:35,045 --> 00:17:37,187
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there are certain things that you kind of know obviously are wrong.

266
00:17:38,127 --> 00:17:43,111
[SPEAKER_01]: But I would take those books, because it's not enough just, oh, this is wrong, because it's a different tradition.

267
00:17:44,698 --> 00:17:48,099
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to understand, as I start reading it, why this is wrong.

268
00:17:48,279 --> 00:17:51,559
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to explain to myself and to others, if somebody were to ask me.

269
00:17:52,559 --> 00:17:56,900
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, see, Nagarjuna view was wrong for such and such reason.

270
00:17:58,160 --> 00:17:59,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, oh, he's wrong.

271
00:18:00,721 --> 00:18:06,982
[SPEAKER_01]: If he's wrong, means he might, he must be contradicting at some point, some aspect of the teaching.

272
00:18:07,742 --> 00:18:09,542
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm not like fishing for... No.

273
00:18:09,683 --> 00:18:11,823
[SPEAKER_01]: If you understand it fully, because he might be right.

274
00:18:12,578 --> 00:18:14,779
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's frightening, especially in the beginning.

275
00:18:14,979 --> 00:18:16,961
[SPEAKER_01]: It fills you with anxiety, because, oh, this is wrong.

276
00:18:17,981 --> 00:18:20,683
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you start thinking, well, what if I don't see it as wrong?

277
00:18:22,344 --> 00:18:23,644
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, yeah, that's the real risk.

278
00:18:23,704 --> 00:18:26,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Means then, what you think you understood, you haven't.

279
00:18:28,447 --> 00:18:35,951
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't run away from that anxiety, and accept it, and then read through it, then you realize, actually, that is the work.

280
00:18:36,832 --> 00:18:42,355
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm undoing my own mistaken notions, through understanding wrong view as wrong view.

281
00:18:44,655 --> 00:18:49,678
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm developing my own correct notions by understanding right view as right view.

282
00:18:50,579 --> 00:18:54,822
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I were looking for faults, ah, that's it.

283
00:18:55,542 --> 00:18:58,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Or just read the moment it displeases me, it's wrong.

284
00:18:58,905 --> 00:19:02,127
[SPEAKER_01]: I would never understand wrong is wrong, which means I would never understand right is right.

285
00:19:02,487 --> 00:19:05,489
[SPEAKER_00]: And your criteria for right and wrong is just how you feel.

286
00:19:05,589 --> 00:19:06,890
[SPEAKER_01]: It's fully emotional, yeah.

287
00:19:06,910 --> 00:19:08,271
[SPEAKER_01]: As always, you haven't changed.

288
00:19:08,551 --> 00:19:09,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

289
00:19:09,392 --> 00:19:13,655
[SPEAKER_01]: You might have refined it, but fundamentally it's still simply based in how you feel.

290
00:19:14,800 --> 00:19:16,242
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's not the criteria.

291
00:19:16,262 --> 00:19:17,283
[SPEAKER_00]: I like Theravāda.

292
00:19:17,323 --> 00:19:18,424
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the criteria, yeah.

293
00:19:18,604 --> 00:19:19,745
[SPEAKER_01]: And why do you like Theravāda?

294
00:19:19,785 --> 00:19:24,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't know, because the first retreat I went was Theravāda, and I liked the feeling, and that's it.

295
00:19:25,090 --> 00:19:26,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely circumstantial.

296
00:19:26,632 --> 00:19:29,615
[SPEAKER_01]: And, by the way, for the same reason, people like the non-Theravāda.

297
00:19:29,635 --> 00:19:30,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, completely.

298
00:19:30,756 --> 00:19:34,480
[SPEAKER_01]: They went into that direction and, like, oh no, this is... this is my path.

299
00:19:35,060 --> 00:19:36,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what is the path?

300
00:19:36,141 --> 00:19:37,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Path is that which frees you from suffering.

301
00:19:38,618 --> 00:19:40,959
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you can be in Theravāda and not be on the path.

302
00:19:41,179 --> 00:19:43,180
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be non-Theravāda and not be on the path.

303
00:19:43,680 --> 00:19:48,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, the only way you'll be on the path is if you actually are understanding the dhamma.

304
00:19:49,543 --> 00:19:56,105
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have that right criteria, whereby understanding of wrong is wrong and right is right, frees you from suffering.

305
00:19:57,426 --> 00:19:59,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Wrong view is wrong view, right view is right view.

306
00:19:59,747 --> 00:20:00,687
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the Sotāpanna.

307
00:20:01,788 --> 00:20:03,748
[SPEAKER_01]: And to what extent Sotāpanna is free from suffering?

308
00:20:03,788 --> 00:20:04,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the Buddha said himself.

309
00:20:05,409 --> 00:20:07,030
[SPEAKER_01]: The mountain Himalayas,

310
00:20:08,138 --> 00:20:09,739
[SPEAKER_01]: And seven grains of sand are left.

311
00:20:12,582 --> 00:20:15,605
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how much suffering Sotāpanna has abolished.

312
00:20:16,525 --> 00:20:18,527
[SPEAKER_01]: The mountains of Himalayas suffering he's abandoned.

313
00:20:18,787 --> 00:20:20,689
[SPEAKER_01]: All he has left is these seven grains of sand.

314
00:20:20,849 --> 00:20:21,710
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how much he has left.

315
00:20:22,110 --> 00:20:24,112
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see how profound right view is.

316
00:20:25,934 --> 00:20:32,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can take that further and abandon these seven grains of sand as well, become an arahant, and you have to, eventually.

317
00:20:34,103 --> 00:20:41,705
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, right view is not a joke, and it's not something you get on account of information, but at the same time, it's not this impossibility either.

318
00:20:42,265 --> 00:20:43,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have your senses intact?

319
00:20:43,366 --> 00:20:44,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

320
00:20:44,066 --> 00:20:45,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you, like, intellectually sound?

321
00:20:47,107 --> 00:20:47,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

322
00:20:47,787 --> 00:20:48,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you think for yourself?

323
00:20:48,787 --> 00:20:48,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

324
00:20:49,427 --> 00:20:50,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you restrain yourself?

325
00:20:50,727 --> 00:20:51,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, of course.

326
00:20:51,848 --> 00:20:52,848
[SPEAKER_01]: You're aware of your choices?

327
00:20:53,948 --> 00:20:55,729
[SPEAKER_01]: No excuse, then, for not getting the right view.

328
00:20:59,230 --> 00:20:59,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Suttas are there.

329
00:21:01,933 --> 00:21:03,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Read it, ponder upon it.

330
00:21:03,694 --> 00:21:07,577
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you realize the thing that impedes you is what you just read.

331
00:21:07,597 --> 00:21:12,560
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not the lack of instruction, or this, or, oh, they're making me do these things.

332
00:21:12,720 --> 00:21:12,880
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

333
00:21:13,640 --> 00:21:20,024
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your lack of faith, looking for faults, lack of respect, lack of application.

334
00:21:22,746 --> 00:21:26,428
[SPEAKER_01]: If you carry on, you realize, ah, so it's this thing, and they're fully dependent upon you.

335
00:21:27,549 --> 00:21:29,390
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can then choose to abandon it.

336
00:21:31,077 --> 00:21:31,658
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just read it.

337
00:21:32,058 --> 00:21:36,422
[SPEAKER_00]: It says here, he pays respects, he gives ear, he listens to what's being said.

338
00:21:38,583 --> 00:21:39,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Then he hears the dhamma.

339
00:21:40,104 --> 00:21:42,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Having heard the dhamma, he memorizes it.

340
00:21:43,367 --> 00:21:45,589
[SPEAKER_00]: He examines the meaning of the teachings.

341
00:21:45,729 --> 00:21:46,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Examines the meaning.

342
00:21:47,010 --> 00:21:51,213
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, how often that gets overlooked today.

343
00:21:51,233 --> 00:21:51,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, knowing.

344
00:21:51,874 --> 00:21:52,895
[SPEAKER_01]: The knowing is now.

345
00:21:52,975 --> 00:21:54,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Now I know that I know.

346
00:21:54,696 --> 00:21:55,237
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you know?

347
00:21:55,457 --> 00:21:56,258
[SPEAKER_01]: What have you understood?

348
00:21:56,418 --> 00:21:57,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Please, explain to me.

349
00:21:57,599 --> 00:21:58,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I can't put it into words.

350
00:21:59,100 --> 00:22:00,461
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no understanding then.

351
00:22:01,816 --> 00:22:08,778
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, the way the monks back in the day would know whether they are kind of on the right path or not, is they get other monks to question them.

352
00:22:09,418 --> 00:22:10,279
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they explain.

353
00:22:10,619 --> 00:22:14,380
[SPEAKER_01]: And the ones who understood would say, yeah, yeah, what you're saying is basically right.

354
00:22:15,180 --> 00:22:16,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they understood it.

355
00:22:16,080 --> 00:22:17,561
[SPEAKER_01]: They know what this person's talking about.

356
00:22:18,721 --> 00:22:20,001
[SPEAKER_01]: So examine the meaning.

357
00:22:20,021 --> 00:22:21,482
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing mystical about it.

358
00:22:23,122 --> 00:22:24,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I had this experience and, you know, just...

359
00:22:26,136 --> 00:22:27,057
[SPEAKER_01]: It's out of this world.

360
00:22:27,157 --> 00:22:27,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, great.

361
00:22:27,737 --> 00:22:28,638
[SPEAKER_01]: What is out of this world?

362
00:22:28,678 --> 00:22:29,599
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean by experience?

363
00:22:29,619 --> 00:22:33,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Is the Buddha teaching that liberation comes out of the experience from out of this world?

364
00:22:33,842 --> 00:22:36,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Or comes out of full understanding upon examining the meaning?

365
00:22:37,344 --> 00:22:39,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, full understanding upon examining the meaning.

366
00:22:39,085 --> 00:22:40,586
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't matter what experience you have.

367
00:22:40,806 --> 00:22:42,367
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is whether you understood it or not.

368
00:22:45,109 --> 00:22:46,210
[SPEAKER_01]: So, examining the meaning.

369
00:22:46,710 --> 00:22:47,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you examining the meaning?

370
00:22:49,003 --> 00:22:52,085
[SPEAKER_01]: After you lend your ear, not looking for faults, out of faith.

371
00:22:52,585 --> 00:23:06,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so now, day in, day out, my practice is examining the meaning to the point of understanding these universal truths, the dhamma, that frees me from liability to suffer, let alone any suffering right here, right now.

372
00:23:06,994 --> 00:23:07,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Because without that...

373
00:23:11,902 --> 00:23:15,765
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot gain a reflective acceptance of those teachings.

374
00:23:16,005 --> 00:23:19,629
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't gain an understanding if you do not examine the meaning.

375
00:23:19,869 --> 00:23:20,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

376
00:23:20,790 --> 00:23:22,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Just magically, somehow...

377
00:23:22,731 --> 00:23:23,432
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot occur.

378
00:23:24,753 --> 00:23:26,735
[SPEAKER_00]: So, after gaining an understanding,

379
00:23:28,239 --> 00:23:29,220
[SPEAKER_01]: You know what needs to be done.

380
00:23:29,800 --> 00:23:30,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Zeal.

381
00:23:30,380 --> 00:23:31,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Effort springs up.

382
00:23:31,681 --> 00:23:33,782
[SPEAKER_00]: When effort springs up, he applies his will.

383
00:23:34,443 --> 00:23:34,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

384
00:23:35,043 --> 00:23:36,043
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands what needs to be done.

385
00:23:36,063 --> 00:23:39,986
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can think about, like, oh, you need to do... you need to fix something.

386
00:23:40,526 --> 00:23:44,128
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have this idea that you need to fix, but you have no clue how to go about it.

387
00:23:45,269 --> 00:23:46,910
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you feel towards that prospect?

388
00:23:47,330 --> 00:23:51,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, like... Because you don't really know what you need to do.

389
00:23:52,233 --> 00:23:53,253
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know how to fix it.

390
00:23:53,914 --> 00:23:55,595
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't even know exactly what needs fixing.

391
00:23:56,255 --> 00:23:57,376
[SPEAKER_01]: But then imagine, ah...

392
00:23:58,174 --> 00:24:00,576
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what needs fixing, and this is how you do it.

393
00:24:02,257 --> 00:24:02,857
[SPEAKER_00]: I do it right now.

394
00:24:04,639 --> 00:24:06,360
[SPEAKER_01]: You immediately get inspiration to do it.

395
00:24:07,401 --> 00:24:19,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now you know what needs to be done, and you know what needs to be done, because you have reflexively understood what needs to be done, and you have reflexively understood what needs to be done, because you made the effort to understand the meaning of what needs to be done.

396
00:24:22,191 --> 00:24:23,932
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like I need to find the zeal now.

397
00:24:24,112 --> 00:24:26,514
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be the result of your understanding.

398
00:24:26,534 --> 00:24:26,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

399
00:24:27,355 --> 00:24:27,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

400
00:24:32,312 --> 00:24:33,192
[SPEAKER_00]: The path is clear.

401
00:24:35,334 --> 00:24:38,235
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have no zeal, it means you lack understanding.

402
00:24:40,556 --> 00:24:41,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

403
00:24:41,697 --> 00:24:45,839
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you do not have energy for practice, it means you haven't understood it enough.

404
00:24:48,220 --> 00:24:50,921
[SPEAKER_01]: So, oh, but I can't practice because I don't have the energy.

405
00:24:50,981 --> 00:24:53,102
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you don't have the energy because you're not practicing.

406
00:24:54,890 --> 00:25:02,258
[SPEAKER_01]: You will get the energy if you start making the effort to understand, which will then start increasing the clarity of what needs to be done.

407
00:25:03,279 --> 00:25:11,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Energy will be the result of it.

408
00:25:11,988 --> 00:25:17,314
[SPEAKER_00]: A lot of people actually say that I don't have the energy for practice anymore.

409
00:25:19,111 --> 00:25:20,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you're... You've never had it.

410
00:25:20,792 --> 00:25:21,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

411
00:25:22,413 --> 00:25:24,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Because energy, vidya, properly, is effortless.

412
00:25:26,495 --> 00:25:31,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Based upon mindfulness, it's the result of your correct seeing and understanding the meaning that you pondered upon.

413
00:25:32,758 --> 00:25:34,038
[SPEAKER_00]: The result of having... That's what it is.

414
00:25:34,059 --> 00:25:35,379
[SPEAKER_01]: A tireless energy arose in me.

415
00:25:36,380 --> 00:25:36,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

416
00:25:37,407 --> 00:25:42,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even when they just talk on dhamma, the result of just that talk would be tireless energy.

417
00:25:42,290 --> 00:25:42,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's what it says.

418
00:25:43,491 --> 00:25:44,292
[SPEAKER_00]: He applies his will.

419
00:25:44,352 --> 00:25:48,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Having applied his will, he scrutinizes, he resolutely strives.

420
00:25:49,655 --> 00:25:51,336
[SPEAKER_00]: That whole tirelessly striving.

421
00:25:51,356 --> 00:25:57,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Tireless, yeah.

422
00:25:57,200 --> 00:26:00,943
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't have a clear sight of what needs to be done.

423
00:26:01,723 --> 00:26:03,444
[SPEAKER_01]: To that extent, you don't have energy.

424
00:26:05,963 --> 00:26:10,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you see the path that needs to be walked, to freedom... You'll be running.

425
00:26:11,184 --> 00:26:13,184
[SPEAKER_00]: For freedom from suffering, you'll be running.

426
00:26:18,265 --> 00:26:29,807
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the same, for the same reason, I mean, a slight kind of detail, but for the same reason, it's said that a noble disciple does not free himself from sensuality.

427
00:26:29,847 --> 00:26:33,968
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like Sotāpanna Sakadāgāmī doesn't quite become anāgāmī yet, because he lacks faith.

428
00:26:35,805 --> 00:26:36,445
[SPEAKER_01]: To that extent.

429
00:26:37,445 --> 00:26:38,486
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of...

430
00:26:38,506 --> 00:26:56,869
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he has understood that he got the right view, understood the way out of suffering, but there are still these residues of sensuality, ill-will, that now he needs to clarify the meaning of those even more, and if he does so, the energy, resoluteness towards overcoming sensuality will be the result of it.

431
00:26:57,870 --> 00:27:04,271
[SPEAKER_01]: But he doesn't do that because he doesn't really develop faith in those directions, so to speak.

432
00:27:05,427 --> 00:27:17,113
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you found a doctor, he cured you, you see for yourself now what health is, but there's still some lingering symptoms that pop up, bother you, disappear.

433
00:27:18,134 --> 00:27:23,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, from your overall view of general health, you're not terminal anymore.

434
00:27:23,297 --> 00:27:24,617
[SPEAKER_01]: This is nothing.

435
00:27:24,798 --> 00:27:28,019
[SPEAKER_01]: But, from the point of view of itself, it's still something.

436
00:27:28,520 --> 00:27:30,641
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still these ailments that come up.

437
00:27:32,182 --> 00:27:34,563
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you just need to develop faith a bit further,

438
00:27:35,795 --> 00:27:37,336
[SPEAKER_01]: And start treating those ailments as well.

439
00:27:37,616 --> 00:27:48,705
[SPEAKER_01]: So that the energy... Then, once you get to know how to deal with the ailments, on account of reflecting upon it, thinking upon it, the energy will be effortless.

440
00:27:48,725 --> 00:27:51,747
[SPEAKER_00]: But isn't... isn't that with all life's goals?

441
00:27:51,927 --> 00:27:52,088
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

442
00:27:52,128 --> 00:27:53,048
[SPEAKER_00]: As soon as you see it.

443
00:27:53,068 --> 00:27:54,429
[SPEAKER_01]: This is... ah, that's how you do it.

444
00:27:54,469 --> 00:27:54,990
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

445
00:27:55,010 --> 00:27:58,693
[SPEAKER_01]: You see that in those, like, you know, genetic sort of motivational quotes.

446
00:27:58,733 --> 00:27:59,373
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always that.

447
00:27:59,573 --> 00:28:01,575
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just people don't even think about it.

448
00:28:01,715 --> 00:28:02,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if you think about it,

449
00:28:03,982 --> 00:28:04,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait a minute.

450
00:28:04,542 --> 00:28:06,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I could actually do this.

451
00:28:06,343 --> 00:28:07,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarity of your...

452
00:28:07,744 --> 00:28:11,145
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you think, the more you ponder, the clearer it becomes what needs to be done.

453
00:28:13,146 --> 00:28:14,586
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, it starts with that attitude.

454
00:28:19,348 --> 00:28:22,890
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why it's also important to come across the right instruction that tells you this.

455
00:28:23,770 --> 00:28:27,592
[SPEAKER_01]: If I just tell you, oh, no, practice of dhamma, it's about non-understanding.

456
00:28:28,452 --> 00:28:30,333
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you practice, the less you know.

457
00:28:33,227 --> 00:28:40,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, you know, I heard people say, as a teaching, like, that's completely the opposite.

458
00:28:43,533 --> 00:28:46,514
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, okay, so it's about not knowing.

459
00:28:46,995 --> 00:28:49,556
[SPEAKER_01]: So I must start forgetting even things I know.

460
00:28:50,357 --> 00:28:59,782
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you think that's what the practice is, well, you found a really bad doctor, who's not a doctor, who's not just not curing you, he's basically making you more sick.

461
00:29:01,571 --> 00:29:12,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now, you think that's what dhamma is, and you spend the rest of your life applying the non-knowing of whatever else contradictions you picked up, thinking you're practicing.

462
00:29:13,881 --> 00:29:16,303
[SPEAKER_00]: But we haven't, if you reflected properly enough...

463
00:29:16,323 --> 00:29:17,784
[SPEAKER_01]: If you reflected properly enough, yeah.

464
00:29:18,004 --> 00:29:18,565
[SPEAKER_00]: You would see that.

465
00:29:18,625 --> 00:29:21,447
[SPEAKER_00]: If you just started reading the suttas, you'd see, wait, this doesn't make sense.

466
00:29:21,647 --> 00:29:21,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

467
00:29:22,608 --> 00:29:28,193
[SPEAKER_01]: But some people... Yeah, maybe he meant this, maybe it doesn't matter, I will not listen to that doctor, because he's not careful with his instruction.

468
00:29:30,444 --> 00:29:33,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, oh, yes, yes, I meant this, but I said the opposite.

469
00:29:34,786 --> 00:29:37,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Which self-respecting doctor would do that?

470
00:29:38,208 --> 00:29:39,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, don't go out in the sun.

471
00:29:40,349 --> 00:29:41,590
[SPEAKER_01]: So the heat can harm you.

472
00:29:42,410 --> 00:29:44,391
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, instead, I tell you, there is no heat.

473
00:29:45,852 --> 00:29:46,752
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no cold.

474
00:29:47,333 --> 00:29:48,033
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nothing.

475
00:29:48,193 --> 00:29:50,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Just be here now.

476
00:29:51,215 --> 00:29:54,136
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is heat, and there is cold, and there are the effects of it.

477
00:29:54,236 --> 00:29:56,277
[SPEAKER_01]: They could make your illness worse or better.

478
00:29:57,198 --> 00:29:57,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well.

479
00:29:59,668 --> 00:30:02,529
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not a doctor you should listen to, because he doesn't know what he's talking about.

480
00:30:02,870 --> 00:30:05,711
[SPEAKER_01]: He himself, obviously, is not cured.

481
00:30:05,731 --> 00:30:08,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if he were, why would he tell you the opposite?

482
00:30:10,874 --> 00:30:18,798
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's impossible to do the dhamma rightly, practice correctly, understood rightly, and not get the results of it, not be cured.

483
00:30:19,578 --> 00:30:23,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the simile of the the chick with the hen that sits on its eggs.

484
00:30:24,990 --> 00:30:32,637
[SPEAKER_01]: If the hand sits on her eggs, even if she doesn't wish for her chicks to come up and pierce out safely, they will, because she's doing the work.

485
00:30:33,577 --> 00:30:40,283
[SPEAKER_01]: The other hand doesn't sit on the eggs, even if she does wish, may my chicks pierce out safely into this wall, they won't, because she's not sitting on the eggs.

486
00:30:41,824 --> 00:30:46,308
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're sitting on the eggs, in terms of if you're doing the practice, the result will have to come up.

487
00:30:48,470 --> 00:30:50,131
[SPEAKER_00]: Results of freedom from suffering.

488
00:30:50,372 --> 00:30:51,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Freedom from liability to suffer.

489
00:30:52,538 --> 00:30:52,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

490
00:30:52,858 --> 00:30:54,779
[SPEAKER_01]: The results, they're the results in the suttas.

491
00:30:54,799 --> 00:30:57,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, oh, I feel much better about myself.

492
00:30:57,401 --> 00:30:58,922
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the result of the dhamma.

493
00:30:59,162 --> 00:30:59,302
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

494
00:30:59,482 --> 00:31:01,683
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just the result of you making some sort of effort.

495
00:31:02,164 --> 00:31:02,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is great.

496
00:31:03,284 --> 00:31:07,787
[SPEAKER_01]: But not the threshold, not the bar you need to be... you want to be striving for.

